      <header class="cod-title-block">
        <p class="cod-chapter-label">Chapter Seven · Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits?</p>
        <h1 class="cod-title">Children's Church</h1>
        <p class="cod-subtitle">Go Now, Ye That Are Men</p>
        <p class="cod-tagline">Every church says the family comes first. Then it sends the children down the hall. Pharaoh made the same offer to Moses.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="cod-pullquote">Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits? Let Scripture and history decide.</p>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">INTRODUCTION</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Question No One Is Asking</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">A family walks through the church doors together on Sunday morning, and within two minutes it is taken apart. The baby goes to the nursery. The six-year-old goes to children's church, with puppets, snacks, and a video. The fourteen-year-old goes to the youth room, where the music is louder, the lights are lower, and a youth pastor in his twenties teaches him what to believe. The mother and father walk into the sanctuary alone and sit through a service their children will never hear.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Many churches print it in their statement of belief: parents are the primary teachers of their children. Then the church builds a system in which the parent never teaches his child anything, because someone down the hall is paid to do it. In Scripture, the children stood in the assembly with their parents and heard the Word read aloud. Separating them by age, with their own rooms, teachers, music, and culture, came later, and the record of how it came is open.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture names the power behind a practice that turns believers from the truth: "some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits" (1 Timothy 4:1). Against it stands the Spirit of truth, who "will guide you into all truth" (John 16:13). In the altar call, the enemy erased the blood. <strong>In children's church, he took the children</strong> — out of the father's hands, and onto another potter's wheel.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">I</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">A School for Children With No One (1780-1900)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The first Sunday school was not a church program. In 1780, in the English city of Gloucester, a newspaper publisher named Robert Raikes looked at the children of the poor. Most worked six days a week in the mills and factories. On Sunday, their one day off, they ran wild in the streets. Many of their parents could not read either. Raikes hired women to gather these children on Sunday and teach them to read, using the Bible as the reading book. Within fifty years more than a million children in Britain were attending Sunday schools. It was a rescue: a charity for children who had no one at home to teach them.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Caring for the child with no father is the heart of Yahuah's Law. He is "a father of the fatherless" (Psalm 68:5), and the Law commands that the fatherless be fed and protected (Deuteronomy 14:29). Raikes's first schools did that, and that is the Spirit of truth at work.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Then the idea crossed the ocean and moved into the churches. By the late 1800s the Sunday school was for every child in the congregation, held in its own rooms, divided by age, with printed lessons and its own teachers. The father who once taught his own children now dropped them at a classroom door. A remedy built for children with no father became the standard for children who had one, and the father was relieved of the one job Yahuah gave him by name: "thou shalt teach them diligently unto thy children" (Deuteronomy 6:7). <strong>A practice that turns every child into a fatherless child, one Sunday at a time, is a seducing spirit's work. It does not serve the fatherless. It makes more of them.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Robert Raikes and the Sunday School</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">Robert Raikes was born in Gloucester in 1736 and inherited his father's newspaper, the <em>Gloucester Journal</em>. He spent years visiting the city jail and became convinced that crime began in childhood, among children who grew up with no schooling and no one to guide them.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">In 1780, with the help of a local clergyman, Thomas Stock, he hired women in a poor part of the city to teach children on Sundays. The children came at ten in the morning, learned to read, went to church, returned for more lessons, and were sent home in the evening. Many were factory children whose only free day was Sunday. Discipline was strict, and children were expected to come with clean hands and combed hair.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Raikes published accounts of the schools in his newspaper in 1783, and the idea spread quickly. In 1785 a Sunday School Society was formed in London to start schools across the country. By the 1830s more than a million children in Britain were enrolled. Some critics opposed the schools, saying it was wrong to teach on Sunday, and others feared that teaching the poor to read would make them restless.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">In America the schools moved inside the churches. The American Sunday School Union was founded in 1824 and sent workers across the frontier to start Sunday schools. In 1872 a national convention adopted the Uniform Lesson plan, so that every Sunday school in the country studied the same passage on the same Sunday. In 1908 graded lessons followed, with a separate course for each age group. By then the Sunday school was no longer a rescue for children with no one. It was a department of the church, with its own superintendent, its own rooms, and its own printed curriculum.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Timothy learned the Scriptures "from a child" at home, from his grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice (2 Timothy 1:5; 3:15). Paul gave the teaching of children to the fathers: "bring them up in the nurture and admonition of the Master" (Ephesians 6:4).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">II</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Whoever Shapes the Child (1763-1913)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">At the same time, the governments of the world were learning what the church was about to learn: whoever teaches the children shapes the nation. In 1763 Frederick the Great ordered every child in the Prussian countryside into school from age five to thirteen. After Prussia's defeat by Napoleon, the philosopher Johann Fichte called for a new state education in his addresses to the German nation (1807-1808), and he said plainly what it was for: the student must be fashioned so that "he simply cannot will otherwise than what you wish him to will."</p>

      <p class="cod-body">In 1843 the American school reformer Horace Mann traveled to Prussia and came home full of praise for its schools. In 1852 Massachusetts passed the first compulsory school attendance law in the United States, and other states followed. In 1902 John D. Rockefeller funded the General Education Board, which poured millions into American schools. Its chairman, Frederick Gates, wrote in 1913: "In our dream, we have limitless resources, and the people yield themselves with perfect docility to our molding hand." The words "our molding hand" are his.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture has already named the molding hand. Yahuah sent Jeremiah to the potter's house and said, "as the clay is in the potter's hand, so are ye in mine hand" (Jeremiah 18:6). "We are the clay, and thou our potter; and we all are the work of thy hand" (Isaiah 64:8). Under that hand, Yahuah gave the shaping of a child to the father: He "commanded our fathers, that they should make them known to their children" (Psalm 78:5). This is the order of the Spirit of truth.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Every power in Scripture that wanted to own Yahuah's people went for the children first. Pharaoh ordered the Hebrew boys killed (Exodus 1:22). Herod did the same at Bethlehem (Matthew 2:16). Babylon took a gentler road. Nebuchadnezzar chose the brightest young men of Judah, gave them three years of Babylon's learning and language, fed them from the king's table, and changed their names (Daniel 1:3-7). Daniel, "El is my judge," became Belteshazzar, named for Babylon's god. Hananiah, "Yah is gracious," and Azariah, "Yah has helped," lost the name of Yah altogether. <strong>Babylon did not need to kill the children. It only needed to teach them.</strong> A man who dreams of a people that "yield themselves with perfect docility to our molding hand" is reaching for the Potter's place. "Woe unto him that striveth with his Maker!" (Isaiah 45:9). <strong>Only one Potter has the right to the clay, and a seducing spirit always wants His wheel.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Fichte, Mann, and the Graded Classroom</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Prussia.</strong> In 1763 Frederick the Great issued a general school order for the Prussian countryside requiring children to attend school from about age five until they could read and know their catechism, usually around thirteen. After Napoleon crushed the Prussian army in 1806, the philosopher Johann Gottlieb Fichte gave his <em>Addresses to the German Nation</em> in Berlin in 1807 and 1808. He argued that the old schools had failed because they only advised the student, leaving him free to choose. The new education, he said, must shape the will itself, so that the student "simply cannot will otherwise" than what his teachers wish. Prussia built a national system of state schools, teacher training colleges, and required attendance over the following decades.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Horace Mann.</strong> In 1837 Horace Mann became the first secretary of the new Massachusetts Board of Education. In 1843 he toured schools in Europe, and in his <em>Seventh Annual Report</em> he praised the Prussian schools above all others: their trained teachers, their order, and their division of children by age and ability. American reformers began to copy them.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The graded classroom.</strong> In 1848 the Quincy Grammar School opened in Boston with a separate classroom for each grade, a teacher for each room, and a desk for each pupil. It became the model for American public schools. Children were now sorted by age, moved through grades one year at a time, and taught by a stranger chosen by the state.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The law.</strong> In 1852 Massachusetts passed the first compulsory attendance law in the United States. Other states followed one by one. By 1918 every state had one.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The board.</strong> In 1902 John D. Rockefeller funded the General Education Board, which gave many millions to schools, colleges, and farm education in the American South. In 1913 its chairman, Frederick Gates, described the board's vision in a paper called <em>The Country School of To-Morrow</em>: "In our dream, we have limitless resources, and the people yield themselves with perfect docility to our molding hand." He wrote that the aim was not to make the farm children into scholars or professionals, but to train them for a good life where they were.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The age-graded room the church built for its children was copied from this school. The Sunday school classroom, divided by age with a teacher at the front, is the Quincy school on a Sunday morning.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">III</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Youth Movement (1881-1950s)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The church soon gave its teenagers their own world too. In 1881 Francis Clark, a Congregational minister in Portland, Maine, founded the Christian Endeavor Society, a meeting just for young people, and the denominations built their own versions. The great leap came in the 1940s. Jim Rayburn founded Young Life in 1941, built on reaching high schoolers outside the church through clubs, camps, and fun. In 1944 Youth for Christ began Saturday night rallies for teenagers with lively music and a sermon, under the slogan "Geared to the times, anchored to the Rock." Its first full-time evangelist was a young preacher named Billy Graham. Youth culture now had its own church.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">In the same years, another nation ran the same idea to its end. The Hitler Youth became the official youth organization of Germany in 1936, and membership was later made compulsory. In a speech in November 1933, as recorded by the historian William Shirer, Hitler said that when a parent told him he would never join the movement, he answered: "Your child belongs to us already." The parent would pass away, but the children were already in the new camp, "and in a short time they will know nothing else."</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Young Life and Youth for Christ were not the Hitler Youth, and nothing in the record says they copied it. They were sincere men trying to reach teenagers. But the same lesson was learned on both sides of the ocean in the same decade: <strong>gather the young apart from their parents, give them their own music, leaders, and world, and in one generation they will know nothing else.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-body">When mothers brought their little ones to Yahushua, it was His disciples who tried to send the children away. Yahushua "was much displeased," and said, "Suffer the little children to come unto me, and forbid them not" (Mark 10:13-14). He took them up in His arms in front of everyone (Mark 10:16). Babylon's method in Daniel 1 was the reverse: separate the young, give them their own teachers, their own food, a new culture, and a new name. Daniel refused the king's table (Daniel 1:8). Paul named what the last days would bring: men "disobedient to parents" (2 Timothy 3:2). <strong>A seducing spirit does not need children to hate their fathers. It only needs them to grow up in a different room.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Young Life, Youth for Christ, and the Youth Pastor</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Young Life.</strong> Jim Rayburn was a Presbyterian youth worker in Texas who found that most high school students never came to church. In 1941 he founded Young Life in Dallas to go to them instead: weekly clubs in homes, games, skits, singing, a short talk, and summer camps. He is remembered for the saying that "it's a sin to bore a kid." Young Life leaders were trained to earn the right to be heard by spending time with students at their games and hangouts.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Youth for Christ.</strong> In 1944 Torrey Johnson, a Chicago pastor, began Saturday night rallies for teenagers and servicemen in a downtown hall, with big bands, quartets, testimonies, and a sermon. Youth for Christ International was organized in 1945, and Billy Graham became its first full-time evangelist, traveling the country and Europe. The rallies were built to compete with the dance halls and the movies — to give young people a Christian version of their own culture.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The youth pastor.</strong> After the war, churches began hiring staff just for teenagers. By the 1960s and 1970s the youth pastor was a common position in American churches, usually a young man in his twenties, often fresh out of Bible college. Youth Specialties, founded in 1969, published games, skits, and programming books for youth groups and held national conventions for youth workers. The youth room became its own world, with its own music, its own trips, its own budget, and its own pastor.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The results.</strong> Researchers have studied what happens to these young people after high school. LifeWay Research found in 2007 that about 70 percent of young adults who had attended church regularly as teenagers stopped attending for at least a year between ages 18 and 22, and a 2019 study put the figure at 66 percent. The Fuller Youth Institute, studying youth group graduates, found that a large share drifted from the faith in college. Its researchers found that one of the strongest marks of those who stayed was time spent with adults in the whole congregation, not only with their own age group.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Moses told the fathers of Israel what to do when their sons asked what the commandments meant: "Then thou shalt say unto thy son" (Deuteronomy 6:20-21). The answer came from the father.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">IV</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Children's Church (1950s-Today)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">After World War II, the baby boom filled the churches with children, and the churches built for them: education wings, nurseries, and classrooms graded by age. More and more churches stopped letting the youngest children sit through the service at all. During the sermon they were led out to "children's church," a service of their own with songs, games, crafts, and a short lesson. By the end of the century, a child could attend church every week from birth to graduation without once sitting in the assembly with his father.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Then the children left. Surveys over the last twenty years have found again and again that about two out of three young people who attended church as teenagers stop attending for at least a year after high school. Many never come back. They had never belonged to the assembly. They had belonged to the youth group, and the youth group ended at graduation.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">In Scripture the children were in the room. When the Law was read every seven years, Moses commanded, "Gather the people together, men, and women, and children … that they may hear, and that they may learn" (Deuteronomy 31:12). Joshua read all the Law "before all the congregation of Israel, with the women, and the little ones" (Joshua 8:35). Joel called the solemn assembly to "gather the children, and those that suck the breasts" (Joel 2:16). When Paul's letter was read aloud at Ephesus, it spoke straight to the children sitting there: "Children, obey your parents in the Master" (Ephesians 6:1). That is the assembly of the Spirit of truth.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">When Pharaoh finally agreed to let Israel go and worship, he made one offer: "Go now ye that are men, and serve Yahuah" (Exodus 10:11). Leave the little ones behind. Moses refused: "We will go with our young and with our old, with our sons and with our daughters" (Exodus 10:9). <strong>Pharaoh's offer is the floor plan of the modern church. The men go into the sanctuary. The little ones stay behind, down the hall.</strong> That offer came from the king of Egypt, and the spirit behind it has not changed.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Child Evangelism and the Wordless Book</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The founder.</strong> Jesse Irvin Overholtzer was a California pastor who became convinced that children could and should be brought to a decision for Messiah at a very young age. In 1937 he founded Child Evangelism Fellowship in Chicago. Its main tool became the Good News Club, a weekly after-school meeting for children held in homes and, later, in schools, with songs, a Bible lesson, memory verses, and an invitation to pray to receive Messiah.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The Wordless Book.</strong> CEF's best-known teaching tool is the Wordless Book, a little book of colored pages with no words. The idea is traced to a sermon by the London preacher Charles Spurgeon in 1866, who described a book of three pages: black for sin, red for the blood, and white for a cleansed heart. D. L. Moody is said to have added a gold page for heaven, and a green page for growing was added later. Each page leads the child, step by step, to a prayer of decision.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The schools.</strong> Good News Clubs grew through the twentieth century and spread to many countries. In 2001 the United States Supreme Court ruled in <em>Good News Club v. Milford Central School</em> that a public school could not keep the club out of its buildings after hours, and Good News Clubs moved into public schools across the country. Today CEF reports clubs in thousands of American schools.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The method.</strong> The Good News Club brings the altar call of Chapter Six to the child: a short lesson, a moment of feeling, an invitation, and a prayer, often led by an adult the child met that afternoon, with the parents not in the room. The decision is counted and reported. The child's father may never know it happened.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Scripture's pattern was a father who taught at the table, and a child who asked: "What mean ye by this service?" (Exodus 12:26). The answer came at Pesach, from the father, year after year.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Scripture Shows Instead</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Yahuah built the teaching of children into the home and the feasts. This is the pattern of the Spirit of truth.</p>

      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>From the first father of the faith.</strong> Yahuah said of Abraham, "I know him, that he will command his children and his household after him, and they shall keep the way of Yahuah" (Genesis 18:19).</li>
        <li><strong>At home, all day.</strong> "Thou shalt teach them diligently unto thy children, and shalt talk of them when thou sittest in thine house, and when thou walkest by the way, and when thou liest down, and when thou risest up" (Deuteronomy 6:7).</li>
        <li><strong>At the feasts, by question.</strong> Yahuah built Pesach so the child would ask: "What mean ye by this service?" — and the father answers (Exodus 12:26-27). At the firstborn: "when thy son asketh thee in time to come, saying, What is this? that thou shalt say unto him" (Exodus 13:14). At the Jordan, the twelve stones were set up so that "when your children shall ask their fathers in time to come," the fathers would tell them (Joshua 4:21-22). Every memorial Yahuah built was a question waiting for a father's answer.</li>
        <li><strong>From one generation to the next.</strong> "Teach them thy sons, and thy sons' sons" (Deuteronomy 4:9). Fathers taught their children, "that the generation to come might know them … that they might set their hope in God … and keep his commandments" (Psalm 78:6-7).</li>
        <li><strong>In the apostles' day, the same.</strong> "Bring them up in the nurture and admonition of the Master" (Ephesians 6:4). Timothy knew the Scriptures "from a child" from his grandmother and mother (2 Timothy 1:5; 3:15).</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="cod-body">In every one of these, the teacher is the parent, the classroom is the home, the road, and the feast, the textbook is the Law of Yahuah, and the goal is a child who will "keep his commandments" and teach them to his own children after him.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Is the Harm?</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul said the fight is "against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this world" (Ephesians 6:12), and the enemy has always known where the future of a people lies. The last words of the Old Testament are about this. Yahuah promised to send Elijah to "turn the heart of the fathers to the children, and the heart of the children to their fathers, lest I come and smite the earth with a curse" (Malachi 4:6). The turning of fathers and children toward each other is what stands between the land and the curse. A system that turns them away from each other every week works against the very thing Malachi said must happen. That is a seducing spirit's aim.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture records what happens when one generation does not teach the next. After Joshua's generation died, "there arose another generation after them, which knew not Yahuah, nor yet the works which he had done for Israel" (Judges 2:10). The next verse says, "And the children of Israel did evil in the sight of Yahuah, and served Baalim" (2:11). It took one generation. The figures on young people leaving the church after graduation describe the same thing.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture also records a priest who raised another man's son in the house of Yahuah while his own sons went bad. Eli brought up Samuel at the tabernacle, and Samuel grew into a prophet. But Eli's own sons "knew not Yahuah" (1 Samuel 2:12), and Yahuah told Samuel why judgment was coming on Eli's house: "his sons made themselves vile, and he restrained them not" (1 Samuel 3:13). The man at the front of the house of worship lost his own children. A church can run a fine children's program and still be Eli's house.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The enemy does not need to steal the children. He only needs the fathers to hand them over.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Verdict</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Children's church is not a doctrine. It is a practice, and that is why no one questions it. The record runs in one direction: a charity school for children with no father, turned into the standard for children who had one; a state school built on the Prussian goal of a child who cannot will otherwise, and funded by men who dreamed of a "molding hand"; a youth movement that gave teenagers their own world in the same decade a dictator told parents their children already belonged to him; and a children's church that sends the little ones out of the room while the Word is read. Every piece moved the child further from his father, and every piece came after the apostles. In the altar call, the enemy erased the blood. In children's church, he took the children.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture asks for the opposite: a father who teaches at his own table, a child who asks at the feast, and a whole family standing together when the Word is read.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Revelation shows what the enemy has always been after. "The dragon stood before the woman … for to devour her child as soon as it was born" (Revelation 12:4). He failed. So he went "to make war with the remnant of her seed, which keep the commandments of God, and have the testimony of Yahushua Messiah" (Revelation 12:17). His war has always been against the children, and the children who keep the commandments are the ones he hates most.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>Pharaoh said, leave the little ones. Moses said, we go together. Every Sunday, the church has to choose which one it will answer.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-end"><span class="cod-end-mark">◆</span></div>
    </div>
  </article>
